{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to the EU AI Act, how does the classification of high-risk AI systems depend on both product safety regulations and critical domains?", "answer": "High-risk AI systems are classified if they are regulated by EU product safety regulations or fall within a list of critical domains and use cases in Annex III.", "evidence": [{"page": 3, "quote": "The AI Act establishes two classification criteria for the high-risk level. The first criterion covers products that are already regulated by the EU product safety regulations (Art. 6(1)(a) AIA). The second comprises AI systems that fall within a list of critical domains, as well as the associated use cases, which describe the purpose, the AI capability, deployer, and subject (Golpayegani et al., 2023) (Art. 6(1)(b), Annex III AIA)."}]},
  {"question": "How does the EU AI Act's risk-based approach balance the protection of fundamental rights with the intended purpose of AI systems?", "answer": "The Act balances fundamental rights protection with legitimate purposes, considering both the anticipated risk and the benefits of technological innovation.", "evidence": [{"page": 5, "quote": "The AI Act aims to balance the protection of health, safety, and fundamental rights relative to the purposes pursued by the providers or deployers of a respective AI system, while also considering the benefits of technological innovation (Recitals 4, 5, and 6 AIA) (De Gregorio and Dunn, 2022; Koulu et al., 2023)."}]},
  {"question": "What is the definition of 'risk' in Article 3(2) of the EU AI Act?", "answer": "Risk is defined as 'the combination of the probability of an occurrence of harm and the severity of that harm'.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "The AI Act defines risk as 'the combination of the probability of an occurrence of harm and the severity of that harm' (Art. 3(2))."}]},
  {"question": "Which rights are included under 'fundamental rights' as referenced in the EU AI Act?", "answer": "Fundamental rights include rights in the Charter of Fundamental Rights of the EU, such as human dignity, data protection, freedom of expression, non-discrimination, fair trial, and good administration.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "Fundamental rights include the rights enshrined in the Charter of Fundamental Rights of the EU (CFR), such as the right to human dignity (Art. 1), the protection of personal data (Art. 8), freedom of expression and information (Art. 11), or the right to non-discrimination (Art. 21), but also the right to an effective remedy and to a fair trial (Art. 47), or the right to good administration (Art. 41 and Recital 48 AIA)."}]},
  {"question": "How does the EU AI Act address the challenge of quantifying severity for intangible rights like privacy?", "answer": "The Act notes that intangible rights like privacy are hard to measure, as harms may not leave visible evidence and may involve subjective perceptions.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "Many rights are intangible, such as the right to privacy (Art. 7 CFR), or the right to the protection of personal data (Art. 8 CFR), which makes identifying (let alone, measuring) interference with these rights challenging. Unlike physical injuries or financial losses, privacy harms do not always leave visible evidence."}]},
  {"question": "What risk assessment loophole might arise if AI providers use a frequentist approach under the EU AI Act?", "answer": "Providers may report zero risk if no harmful events are observed, incentivizing them to limit data collection and potentially understate risks.", "evidence": [{"page": 7, "quote": "For instance, an AI provider or deployer may adopt a frequentist view, which incentivises them to intentionally limit the amount of data they collect since no observations of an event result in an estimated probability of 0 for all harms. If harms occur with probability 0, then the risk they pose would also be 0."}]}
]}
